th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/strictly-dominated-strategy.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/strictly-dominated-strategy.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_strictly_dominated_strategy_a40c4dc8_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In a normal-form game $\\langle N,\\{S_i\\},\\{v_i\\}\\rangle$, a strategy $s_i$ is worse than some other strategy of player $i$ against each $s_{-i}$, but the better strategy differs from one $s_{-i}$ to another. Is $s_i$ then strictly dominated?","options":["Yes, since $s_i$ is beaten against every $s_{-i}$ by some strategy.","Not necessarily, since one fixed $s_i'$ must beat $s_i$ against every $s_{-i}$.","Yes, provided the better strategies give strictly higher payoffs.","No, since a strategy is strictly dominated only if it is worse than all others."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e72c81b140fcbb9ce35ba65b","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_strictly_dominated_strategy_a40c4dc8_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A rational player never chooses a strictly dominated strategy. Why would this guarantee fail if, against some $s_{-i}$, the alternative only tied with the strategy instead of beating it?","options":["It would not fail: a strategy that is never better than the alternative is never chosen.","It would fail because ties make the payoff function unable to represent preferences.","It would fail: a player sure of that $s_{-i}$ finds the tied strategy payoff-maximising.","It would fail only if the tie occurs against every $s_{-i}$ simultaneously."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"be1e49485c256ddbb92af507","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}